description:
memory.rs:286. demo_next_id is declared as:

    impl MemoryBackend {
        fn demo_next_id(inner: &mut Inner, type_name: &'static str, account_id: &str) -> Id { ... }
    }

It is called from inside MemoryBackend methods that have already acquired the lock:

    let mut inner = self.inner.lock().unwrap();
    let server_id = Self::demo_next_id(&mut inner, O::TYPE_NAME, account_id.as_ref());

The function does not use any MemoryBackend state. It uses Inner state (via the &mut Inner) and process-global statics (BASE, COUNTER) in the realistic-demo-ids branch.

This is the shape you write when you have not figured out where the method belongs. Idiomatically it should be a method on Inner:

    impl Inner {
        fn demo_next_id(&mut self, type_name: &'static str, account_id: &str) -> Id { ... }
    }

…and called as `inner.demo_next_id(...)`. That makes the receiver explicit (it is Inner state being mutated, not MemoryBackend state) and lets the method use Inner's private fields (objects_ref, len) without going through the explicit parameter.

Engineering cost: low (correctness is fine), but it's the textbook "free function pretending to be a method" anti-pattern. A reader walking the code wonders why this lives under impl MemoryBackend rather than impl Inner.

Same pattern in canonical jmap-mail-server/src/memory.rs:267-291 (demo_next_id with identical free-fn shape) — propagation needed.
